{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Eamonn F. Healy is a professor of chemistry, organic chemistry, and biochemistry at St. Edward's University in Austin, Texas, where his research focuses on the design of structure-activity probes to elucidate enzymatic activity.","title":"Eamonn Healy"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Targets include HIV-1 integrase, the c-Kit and src-abl proteins, and the metalloproteinases associated with CXCL16 shedding.","title":"Eamonn Healy"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He was born in NewCastle West, Co. Limerick Ireland on September 25, 1958.","title":"Eamonn Healy"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He received a doctorate in chemistry in 1984 from the University of Texas at Austin where he was a student of Dr. Michael J. S. Dewar.","title":"Eamonn Healy"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" As a member of the Dewar research group he co-authored Austin Model 1, or AM1, a semi-empirical method for the quantum calculation of molecular electronic structure in computational chemistry.","title":"Eamonn Healy"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" He appears in Richard Linklater's 2001 film "Waking Life" explaining "telescopic" and technological evolution.","title":"Eamonn Healy"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Dr. Healy is married to Shelley Bueche.","title":"Eamonn Healy"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Arne Jørgen Aasen (born October 15, 1939) is a Norwegian chemist and professor "emeritus" at the University of Oslo (UiO), School of Pharmacy.","title":"Arne Jørgen Aasen"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Aasen received his engineering degree in biochemistry and organic chemistry in 1964, and a doctorate (dr. ing.)","title":"Arne Jørgen Aasen"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" in organic chemistry in August 1966 from The Norwegian Institute of Technology, Trondheim (NTH, renamed in 1996 to Norwegian University of Science and Technology, NTNU).","title":"Arne Jørgen Aasen"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In 1972 he defended his thesis for a doctorate (tekn.","title":"Arne Jørgen Aasen"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" dr.) at The Royal Institute of Technology (KTH), Stockholm, Sweden.","title":"Arne Jørgen Aasen"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The University of Texas at Austin (UT, UT Austin, or Texas) is a public research university and the flagship institution of the University of Texas System.","title":"University of Texas at Austin"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Founded in 1881 as "The University of Texas," its campus is located in Austin, Texas, approximately 1 mile (1.6 km) from the Texas State Capitol.","title":"University of Texas at Austin"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" UT Austin was inducted into the Association of American Universities in 1929, becoming only the third university in the American South to be elected.","title":"University of Texas at Austin"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The institution has the nation's eighth-largest single-campus enrollment, with over 50,000 undergraduate and graduate students and over 24,000 faculty and staff.","title":"University of Texas at Austin"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Dieter Enders (born 1946) is an organic chemist who has done work developing asymmetric synthesis, in particular using modified prolines as asymmetric auxiliaries.","title":"Dieter Enders"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The most widely applied of his chiral auxiliaries are the complementary SAMP and RAMP auxiliaries, which allow for asymmetric alpha-alkylation of aldehydes and ketones.","title":"Dieter Enders"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 1974 he obtained his doctorate from the University of Gießen studying under Dieter Seebach and followed this with a postdoc at Harvard University studying with Elias James Corey.","title":"Dieter Enders"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He then moved back to Gießen to obtain his Habilitation in 1979, whereupon he became a lecturer, soon obtaining Professorship in 1980 as Professor of Organic Chemistry at Bonn.","title":"Dieter Enders"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In 1985 he moved to Aachen, where he has remained as Full Professor of Organic Chemistry and Director.","title":"Dieter Enders"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" He is editor-in-chief of Synthesis and is on the advisory boards of many other journals including Letters in Organic Chemistry and SynLett.","title":"Dieter Enders"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":"During his career he has won many awards.","title":"Dieter Enders"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" These include:","title":"Dieter Enders"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lauder William Jones (July 22, 1869 – December 28, 1960) was an American chemist, born at New Richmond, Ohio.","title":"Lauder William Jones"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He was graduated at Williams College in 1892, and received his Ph. D. from the University of Chicago in 1897.","title":"Lauder William Jones"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In the same year, he became an assistant in chemistry at Chicago, where he remained until 1907.","title":"Lauder William Jones"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" From 1907 to 1918, he was professor of chemistry at the University of Cincinnati, and from 1918 to 1920, he was dean of the School of Chemistry at the University of Minnesota, after which he accepted a call to the chair of chemistry at Princeton.","title":"Lauder William Jones"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" He devoted his attention chiefly to organic chemistry and published papers on nitro-paraffin salts, alkyl derivatives of hydroxylamine, preparation of hydroxamic acids from hydroxylamines, electron conception of valence, and preparation of electromeres.","title":"Lauder William Jones"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" During the War he served with the Chemical War Service as its chief of the research section of offense.","title":"Lauder William Jones"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Dr. Jones was the author of "A Laboratory Outline of Organic Chemistry" (1911).","title":"Lauder William Jones"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" He died in Los Angeles, California in 1960.","title":"Lauder William Jones"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Henk Buck (born Dordrecht, 1930) is an organic chemist.","title":"Henk Buck"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He studied at the University of Leiden where he received his PhD in 1959.","title":"Henk Buck"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He got a lectorship at the University in Theoretical Organic Chemistry in 1964.","title":"Henk Buck"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" For his research he received the Golden Medal of the Royal Netherlands Chemical Society in 1967.","title":"Henk Buck"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In 1970 he was appointed as professor of Physical Organic Chemistry and Organic Chemistry at the University of Technology in Eindhoven.","title":"Henk Buck"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Because there was no Chair for Theoretical Chemistry and Biochemistry he gave lectures in organic chemistry, physical organic chemistry, theoretical organic chemistry, biochemistry and biotechnology.","title":"Henk Buck"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" From 1988-1991 he was Dean of the Chemical Faculty.","title":"Henk Buck"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" For his scientific contributions he became a member of the Royal Netherlands Academy of Arts and Sciences in 1979.","title":"Henk Buck"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" During his scientific career he published more than 300 scientific papers spread over a large area of the chemical field.","title":"Henk Buck"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" Under his supervision 43 chemical engineers obtained their PhD.","title":"Henk Buck"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" The end of his career came prematurely because of a publication in Science in 1990 that had to be retracted because of flawed research.","title":"Henk Buck"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: 1881
